{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>To the Molech.</b> A foreign worship whose name is Molech. Scripture mentions it with the definite article, because in Egypt it was known to them. Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 18:21) that it is possible it is Milkom, the abomination of the children of Ammon (II Kings 23:13). This appears correct, for it is written (I Kings 11:7), \"Then Solomon built a high place for Chemosh, the detestation of Moab, and for Molech, the detestation of the children of Ammon,\" and it too was known to them.\n\nRashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 18:21): This is its mode of worship — one hands over his son to the priests, and the priests make two large fires and pass the son with his feet between the two fires. \"You shall not give\" — this is the handing over to the priests; \"to pass to the Molech\" — this is the passing through the fire. This is not correct, for they said in the Gemara, Sanhedrin (64a): if he passed him through with his foot, he is exempt; rather, it is like the bridging of a furnace, meaning he passes him literally through the fire. Scripture mentioned in this matter both giving and passing through fire, because he is not liable until he hands him over to the priests and passes him through the fire, as is explained in Sanhedrin. Likewise, what the Rav said — that he hands him over to the priests and they pass him through — and so he also wrote in his Commentary on tractate Sanhedrin (64a) — does not appear to be so from their language there in the Gemara. Furthermore, how could he be liable for the death penalty in foreign worship [if only the priests perform the act]? And the language of Scripture itself, \"who passes his son or his daughter through the fire\" (Deuteronomy 18:10), is thus interpreted to mean that the father himself passes him through.\n\nBut the matter is thus: the father himself hands him over to the priests in the name of their abomination — and this is what is written, \"gave to the Molech\" (Leviticus 20:3) — just as one does with inner offerings, as it is written, \"and they shall give them to the priest\" (Leviticus 15:14). Perhaps the priests perform a wave-offering or a presentation before the Molech and then return him to the father's hand, and he takes him and passes him through the flame of the fire — and this is \"passes his son or his daughter through the fire.\" And so they said in the Jerusalem Talmud (Sanhedrin 7:9): he is never liable until he hands him over to the priests and they take him and pass him through — meaning, he takes him back from them and passes him through himself.\n\nBut he would not burn him, for they said (Sanhedrin 64a): one who passes himself through is exempt, because he remains alive even after the passing through, and so Rashi explained. Yet despite all this, it appears to me, from the weight of the verses, that the son was for the fire's consumption. For Scripture says (Ezekiel 23:37), \"and even the children they bore to Me they passed through for them as food,\" and there (Leviticus 18:39 [Ezekiel 23:39]) it says, \"and when they slaughtered their children for their idols\" — they would pass him through the fire until he was burned or died in the fire, and that is his slaughter. For this expression, \"they passed them through for them,\" refers to the Molech, and Scripture says of it \"as food\" and mentions slaughter. If so, what they said, \"like the bridging of a furnace,\" means they would pass him over the flame many times and he dies in the flame of the fire. He is liable by Torah law from the moment of the first passing through, from the time the fire takes hold — for example, when the fire seizes one of his limbs. That is why they needed to say that one who passes himself through is exempt; but the fire would consume him, for this was their sacrifice to the Molech.\n\nThe expression \"passes through fire\" is used everywhere for something that burns in fire yet is not consumed, because they place it in the fire and pass it through it and it does not turn to ash as burnt things do — as in the case of Gentile utensils that are purged in fire, of which it is said (Numbers 31:23), \"you shall pass through fire and it shall be clean.\" And it says of the children of Ammon (II Samuel 12:31 [cf. I Chronicles 20:3]), \"and he passed them through the brick kiln,\" meaning he burned them as they make bricks in a kiln. If so, \"passes his son or his daughter through the fire\" (Deuteronomy 18:10) is placing him in the fire such that the flame takes hold, not mere passing through for nothing. And this is the passing through mentioned regarding the Molech, which is in fire according to our Sages (Sanhedrin 64a), who derived by a gezerah shavah — passing through, passing through: just as there (Deuteronomy 18:10) it is in fire, so too here it is in fire; just as here it is to the Molech, so too there it is to the Molech. And a proof for their words: it is written regarding Josiah (II Kings 23:10), \"and he defiled the Topheth, which is in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, so that no one might pass his son or his daughter through the fire to the Molech\" — if so, the passing through mentioned regarding the Molech is in fire. And so it is written regarding Manasseh (II Chronicles 33:6), \"and he passed his sons through the fire in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom.\" And I found written regarding Ahaz (II Kings 16:3), \"and even his son he passed through the fire, like the abominations of the nations whom YHWH had driven out from before the children of Israel,\" while in Chronicles (II Chronicles 28:3) it says, \"and he burned his sons in fire, like the abominations of the nations whom He had driven out\" — if so, passing through is literally burning in fire.\n\nWhat Scripture says (II Kings 17:31), \"and the Sepharvites burned their children in fire to Adrammelech and Anammelech, the gods of Sepharvaim,\" is a different foreign worship, whose rite is to burn them completely as a burnt offering. And so they would do for the Baal, as written in Jeremiah (19:2), \"and you shall go out to the Valley of the Son of Hinnom,\" and there (Jeremiah 19:4–5) it is written, \"and they filled this place with the blood of the innocent, and they built the high places of the Baal to burn their children in fire as burnt offerings to the Baal, which I did not command, nor did I speak, nor did it come to My mind,\" and it is further written (Jeremiah 32:35), \"and they built the high places of the Baal which are in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, to pass their sons and their daughters through to the Molech, which I did not command them, nor did it come to My mind to do this abomination.\" But since both of these verses speak of the high places of the Baal, and both are in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, and the warning in both is identical — \"which I did not command, nor did I speak, nor did it come to My mind\" — and in that defiled place the Molech was there, as written regarding Josiah, it appears from all this that they would burn their children as burnt offerings to the Molech, which is the Baal, both names connoting kingship and dominion. It is not Baal Peor or Baal Zebub; rather, this one is called \"Baal\" absolutely, just as he is called \"the Molech.\" But Scripture mentions passing through, because the essence of his worship is in passing through from the moment the fire takes hold, and it is called burning because they die there, and therefore they are called burnt offerings.\n\nSo too I believe that \"the gods of Sepharvaim\" (II Kings 17:31) is the Molech, for their epithet contains \"melech\" (king), but both were worshipped: they called one \"Adar\" in a language of honor, and the second \"An\" from the language of afflicting. They combined in both the name \"melech\" (king), and it is like Molech, and the burning is their death in fire as I explained. And if we say these abominations are not the Molech, it is possible that because their worship involved sons and daughters, bringing them into fire in the manner described for the Molech, they combined with them the names \"melech\" and \"Baal,\" yet their worship is not identical: the Molech by passing through, and the gods of Sepharvaim by burning, and the Baal by slaughter and burning as burnt offerings. And it is said that in the Topheth which is in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom there were high places of the Baal, and there too the Molech, so that what Scripture says (Jeremiah 32:34), \"and they built the high places of the Baal which are in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom to pass their children through to the Molech,\" its meaning would be: and to pass their children through to the Molech.\n\nOur Sages said (Sanhedrin 63b): even Hezekiah king of Judah was sought by his father to do thus to him — to burn him in fire — except that his mother smeared him with salamander oil. Now Ahaz passed him through to the Molech, as it is written, \"and even his son he passed through the fire, like the abominations of the nations whom YHWH drove out from before the children of Israel,\" and this is the scriptural language used for the Molech. If so, it is the burning such that the fire takes hold and he dies, and the righteous one was saved by that salamander which the Holy One, blessed be He, created for his sake.\n\nOn the level of peshat they said that passing one's son or daughter through the fire is one of the species of sorcery, for in the context of sorcery it is said (Deuteronomy 18:10), \"there shall not be found among you one who passes his son or his daughter through the fire, a soothsayer, a diviner, an augur, and a sorcerer,\" and it says of Manasseh (II Chronicles 33:6), \"and he passed his sons through the fire in the Valley of the Son of Hinnom, and practiced augury and divination and sorcery, and made a familiar spirit and a wizard,\" and it is written (II Kings 17:17), \"and they passed their sons and their daughters through the fire and practiced soothsaying and divined.\" And the Molech is foreign worship to which they offer the young children. But after we find it written (II Kings 23:10), \"so that no one might pass his son or his daughter through the fire to the Molech,\" we accept the words of our Sages that everything for the Molech is in fire. It is possible, however, that that worship was for them a means of false sorcery, and they would prophesy through it according to their foolishness — they being the prophets of the Baal who offer him sons and daughters, and false dreams and lying divination appear to them. Therefore the Torah associates the matter of the Molech with the matter of sorcery, for here (Leviticus 20:6) it is juxtaposed to \"the person who turns to the familiar spirits and the wizards,\" and in the repetition of the Torah the entire pericope concerns those who prophesy falsely to know the future. \n\nNow Scripture mentions that passing one's seed to the Molech is desecration of the Name, and in the other pericope (Leviticus 20:3) it adds, \"to defile My sanctuary and to profane My holy name.\" Perhaps its reason is: to defile the people sanctified to My name, as I commanded (Leviticus 11:44), \"and you shall sanctify yourselves and be holy, for I am holy\" (Leviticus 21:8), and to profane My holy name by transgressing the grave prohibition before Me, for so it says regarding grave transgressions, \"a man and his father go to the girl, to profane My holy name\" (Amos 2:7). It is also possible that it says this because one who offers his seed to the Molech and afterward comes to the sanctuary of YHWH to offer a sacrifice defiles the sanctuary, for his sacrifices are impure and an abomination to the Name, and he himself is forever impure, having become defiled through the evil he did, as it says regarding the ov and the yidoni, \"to become defiled by them\" (Leviticus 19:31), and as it is written, \"and with their idols they defiled it\" (Ezekiel 36:18). And it mentions desecration of the Name in this connection because when the nations hear that he honored the Molech with his seed and then offers from his animal a sacrifice to the Name, it is a desecration of the Name. And this is what the Prophet said (Ezekiel 23:37–38), \"and even the children they bore to Me they passed through for them as food — and this too they did to Me: they defiled My sanctuary on that day,\" and he said (verse 39), \"and when they slaughtered their children for their idols, they came into My sanctuary on that day to profane it, and behold, thus they did within My house.\"\n\nAnd by way of truth, Israel, the holy seed, are born in the house of HaShem, and this is the meaning of \"whom they bore to Me.\" Now one who offers him to the Molech defiles His sanctuary and profanes His great name, in the language of \"and he shall not profane his seed among his people\" (Leviticus 21:15). Therefore He said of him, \"and I will set My face against that man\" (Leviticus 20:3), \"and I Myself will set My face against that man\" (Leviticus 20:5), and the enlightened will understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban cites several verses with internal parenthetical cross-references (e.g., Ezekiel 23:37–39), some of which he labels with apparent addresses to our chapter (Leviticus 18); these reflect medieval chapter numbering conventions or mnemonic citations and are rendered as they appear in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:52.613382113Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}